// DEFLATE decompressor (RFC 1951) and gzip reader (RFC 1952).
//
// Decoding is the mirror of encoding, with one asymmetry that matters: the decoder is fed bytes it
// did not produce, so every field is untrusted. Bad input must not read out of bounds or loop
// forever. **What it must do instead is the whole of this rewrite**, and it is the subject of a
// closed issue rather than a preference of mine.
//
// ## One entry point, and the trailer is why there were two
//
// Shipped, there are two: `gunzipBytes(u8[] gz)` and
// `gunzipStream(fn[Read()] read, fn[bool(u8[])] write)`. Its README says why, and the reason is not
// laziness:
//
//   > A buffer decode reads the CRC and length from the *last* eight bytes before it starts, which
//   > is how it can pre-size the output — a stream has no last eight bytes until it gets there, so
//   > it checksums as it goes and checks at the end. Same guarantees, opposite order.
//
// A generator collapses them, because a buffer is a one-chunk source, and **the collapse costs the
// pre-sizing**. `gunzipBytes` today allocates the output once at the size the trailer promises; a
// caller of this collects chunks and grows. That is a real regression on the buffer path and it is
// written here rather than in the README's list of wins, because a rewrite that only reports what
// got better is not measuring anything.
//
// It is also recoverable without a second entry point: a caller holding the whole member can read
// its own trailer and size its own `Buf`. The knowledge was never the decoder's; it was the
// decoder's *because* the decoder owned the allocation.
//
// ## `try`, and what `0102` said it would cost
//
// `issues/system/closed/0102` closed on withdrawing a promise — `gunzipStream` cannot tell a caller
// whether the source broke or the archive is corrupt, and now says so. Its resolution wrote the
// shape of a real fix and priced it:
//
//   > **`inflateInto` returns a result rather than trapping on a source failure only** — corruption
//   > keeps trapping — and every read site propagates it. That is a refactor of the decode loop, not
//   > a line.
//   >
//   > Not trapping means threading a status back through every symbol read. A decoder that keeps
//   > going after a failed read with a status nobody checked is how silent corruption gets written,
//   > and this file decodes other people's bytes.
//
// Both halves are answered by one construct and neither is answered by good intentions. `try` is the
// threading, written once per site as one keyword; and *"a status nobody checked"* is not
// expressible, because `Result` is not `void` and a discarded one is a type error. The refactor
// `0102` called a refactor is `try` in front of eleven calls.
//
// **This does not reopen the issue.** `0102` was decided on a reason that has nothing to do with
// cost — *"it should be done for a reason better than closing an argument about an unreachable
// branch"* — and that reason is untouched. What changed is the price, and a price is what was
// written down as the obstacle.
//
// ## And the trap for corruption is argued two different ways
//
// Worth separating, because only one of the two survives.
//
// The **file** justifies it as safety: *"returning data that failed its own checksum would defeat
// the point of carrying one."* That does not distinguish a trap from a `Result` — a caller cannot
// reach the bytes inside an `Err` either, so `Result<Bytes, ChecksumMismatch>` withholds exactly
// what the trap withholds.
//
// The **issue** justifies it as cost, and that one was real: threading a status by hand through
// every symbol read is where the silent-corruption risk lives, not in the return type.
//
// So with `try` the surviving argument is gone, and this decoder returns a `Fault` for corruption
// too. `Corrupt` is a separate arm of the union precisely so a caller can still choose the shipped
// behaviour in one line — `r.orTrap("corrupt gzip member")` — rather than having it chosen for it.

import { Result, Bytes, union } from "core";
import { Fault, SourceFailed, Corrupt, BadMagic, BadMethod, Truncated,
         ChecksumMismatch, LengthMismatch } from "./fault.wac";
import { Window } from "./window.wac";
import { BitReader } from "./bitreader.wac";
import { Decoder } from "./huffman.wac";
import { crc32Finish } from "./crc32.wac";

/**
 * A gzip member, as chunks.
 *
 * `async gen<Bytes> Result<void, Fault>` is the coroutine shape: it yields chunks and *answers*
 * whether it finished. A caller writes one loop and the failure is not a second channel —
 *
 * ```wac
 * try await for (Bytes chunk in gunzip(source)) {
 *   out.pushAll(chunk);
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * — where `try` propagates a `Fault` that the loop's own function must be able to carry. The
 * shipped pair is `fn[Read()] read` and `fn[bool(u8[])] write`, two callbacks and an `i32` status,
 * and the README calls the write callback's `false` *"stopped caring"*. A generator has that for
 * free and does not need a convention for it: a caller that stops iterating stops the machine.
 *
 * **The chunks are views into the decoder's window and are valid until the loop's next step.** See
 * `window.wac` — that rule is the price of not copying, and it is a rule a coroutine can state and a
 * callback pair cannot.
 */
export async gen<Bytes> Result<void, Fault> gunzip(AsyncGen<u8[]> source) {
  BitReader br = BitReader.over(source);

  // Eleven read sites, all of which could fail with a `SourceFailed` the caller wants told apart
  // from a corrupt header, and `0102`'s "every read site propagates it" is what each `try` is.
  i32 m0 = try await br.byte();
  i32 m1 = try await br.byte();
  if (m0 != 0x1F || m1 != 0x8B) { return Err(BadMagic((m1 << 8) | m0)); }

  i32 cm = try await br.byte();
  if (cm != 8) { return Err(BadMethod(cm)); }

  i32 flg = try await br.byte();
  try await br.bits(32);                          // MTIME
  try await br.byte();                            // XFL
  try await br.byte();                            // OS

  if ((flg & 4) != 0) {                           // FEXTRA
    i32 xlen = try await br.bits(16);
    for (i32 i = 0; i < xlen; i++) { try await br.byte(); }
  }
  if ((flg & 8) != 0) { try await br.untilNul(); }    // FNAME
  if ((flg & 16) != 0) { try await br.untilNul(); }   // FCOMMENT
  if ((flg & 2) != 0) { try await br.bits(16); }      // FHCRC

  Window out = Window.create();

  // The block loop. `yield` is the only place this suspends on the output side, and it is between
  // tokens rather than inside one, which is what lets `window.wac` hand out a view of its own
  // storage: nothing writes to that storage until `released` below.
  bool last = false;
  while (!last) {
    last = (try await br.bits(1)) == 1;
    i32 btype = try await br.bits(2);
    try await inflateBlock(br, out, btype);

    while (out.full()) {
      yield out.take();
      out.released();
    }
  }

  try await br.alignByte();
  u32 wantCrc = (try await br.bits(32)) as@ u32;
  i32 wantSize = try await br.bits(32);

  // Everything still held goes out before the check, so a caller that traps on the fault has still
  // seen every byte the member contained. Which is deliberate and is the arguable half: the shipped
  // decoder traps with the tail unemitted. Handing over bytes that then turn out to be wrong is only
  // safe because the fault is the *return*, and a caller cannot finish the loop without seeing it.
  while (out.len > 0) {
    yield out.take();
    out.released();
  }

  u32 got = crc32Finish(out.crc);
  if (got != wantCrc) { return Err(ChecksumMismatch(wantCrc, got)); }
  if (out.total() != wantSize) { return Err(LengthMismatch(wantSize, out.total())); }
  return Ok;
}

/**
 * The whole member, for a caller that has the whole member.
 *
 * Not a second decoder — the two entry points collapsed and this is a four-line caller of the one
 * that is left, which is what "a buffer is a one-chunk source" means in practice.
 */
export async Result<u8[], Fault> gunzipBytes(u8[] gz) {
  Buf b = Buf.create();
  try await for (Bytes chunk in gunzip(AsyncGen.of(gz))) { b.pushAll(chunk); }
  return Ok(b.bytes());
}

async Result<void, Fault> inflateBlock(BitReader br, Window out, i32 btype) { … }
